     The bbiittsseett((99)) family of macros provide a flexible and efficient bitset
     implementation if the maximum size of the set is known at compilation.
     Throughout this manual page, the name _S_E_T_S_I_Z_E refers to the size of the
     bitset in bits.  Individual bits in bitsets are referenced with indices
     zero through _S_E_T_S_I_Z_E _- _1.  One example use of <_s_y_s_/_b_i_t_s_e_t_._h> is
     <_s_y_s_/_c_p_u_s_e_t_._h>.

     These macros are meant to be used in the kernel and are visible if
     _KERNEL is defined when <_s_y_s_/___b_i_t_s_e_t_._h> or <_s_y_s_/_b_i_t_s_e_t_._h> are included in
     a program.  Userland programs must define _WANT_FREEBSD_BITSET before in‐
     cluding these files to make the macros visible.

     The BBIITTSSEETT__DDEEFFIINNEE() macro defines a bitset struct _S_T_R_U_C_T_N_A_M_E with room to
     represent _S_E_T_S_I_Z_E bits.

     The BBIITTSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() macro allows one to initialize a bitset struct
     with a compile time literal value.

     The BBIITTSSEETT__FFSSEETT() macro generates a compile time literal, usable by
     BBIITTSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR(), representing a full bitset (all bits set).  For
     examples of BBIITTSSEETT__TT__IINNIITTIIAALLIIZZEERR() and BBIITTSSEETT__FFSSEETT() usage, see the
     _B_I_T_S_E_T___T___I_N_I_T_I_A_L_I_Z_E_R _E_X_A_M_P_L_E section.  The _N___W_O_R_D_S parameter to
     BBIITTSSEETT__FFSSEETT() should be:

           __bitset_words(SETSIZE)

     The BBIITT__CCLLRR() macro clears bit _b_i_t in the bitset pointed to by _b_i_t_s_e_t.
     The BBIITT__CCLLRR__AATTOOMMIICC() macro is identical, but the bit is cleared atomi‐
     cally.  The BBIITT__TTEESSTT__CCLLRR__AATTOOMMIICC() macro atomically clears the bit and re‐
     turns whether it was set.

     The BBIITT__CCOOPPYY() macro copies the contents of the bitset _f_r_o_m to the bitset
     _t_o.  BBIITT__CCOOPPYY__SSTTOORREE__RREELL() is similar, but copies component machine words
     from _f_r_o_m and writes them to _t_o with atomic store with release semantics.
     (That is, if _t_o is composed of multiple machine words,
     BBIITT__CCOOPPYY__SSTTOORREE__RREELL() performs multiple individually atomic operations.)

     The BBIITT__IISSSSEETT() macro returns true if the bit _b_i_t in the bitset pointed
     to by _b_i_t_s_e_t is set.

     The BBIITT__SSEETT() macro sets bit _b_i_t in the bitset pointed to by _b_i_t_s_e_t.  The
     BBIITT__SSEETT__AATTOOMMIICC() macro is identical, but the bit is set atomically.  The
     BBIITT__SSEETT__AATTOOMMIICC__AACCQQ() macro sets the bit with acquire semantics.  The
     BBIITT__TTEESSTT__SSEETT__AATTOOMMIICC() macro atomically sets the bit and returns whether
     it was set.

     The BBIITT__ZZEERROO() macro clears all bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t.

     The BBIITT__FFIILLLL() macro sets all bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t.

     The BBIITT__SSEETTOOFF() macro clears all bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t before setting only bit
     _b_i_t.

     The BBIITT__EEMMPPTTYY() macro returns true if _b_i_t_s_e_t is empty.

     The BBIITT__IISSFFUULLLLSSEETT() macro returns true if _b_i_t_s_e_t is full (all bits set).

     The BBIITT__FFFFSS() macro returns the 1-index of the first (lowest) set bit in
     _b_i_t_s_e_t, or zero if _b_i_t_s_e_t is empty.  Like with ffs(3), to use the non-
     zero result of BBIITT__FFFFSS() as a _b_i_t index parameter to any other bbiittsseett((99))
     macro, you must subtract one from the result.

     The BBIITT__FFFFSS__AATT() macro returns the 1-index of the first (lowest) set bit
     in _b_i_t_s_e_t, which is greater than the given 1-indexed _s_t_a_r_t, or zero if no
     bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t greater than _s_t_a_r_t are set.

     The BBIITT__FFLLSS() macro returns the 1-index of the last (highest) set bit in
     _b_i_t_s_e_t, or zero if _b_i_t_s_e_t is empty.  Like with fls(3), to use the non-
     zero result of BBIITT__FFLLSS() as a _b_i_t index parameter to any other bbiittsseett((99))
     macro, you must subtract one from the result.

     The BBIITT__FFOORREEAACCHH__IISSSSEETT() macro can be used to iterate over all set bits in
     _b_i_t_s_e_t.  The index variable _b_i_t must have been declared with type _i_n_t,
     and upon each iteration _b_i_t is set to the index of successive set bits.
     The value of _b_i_t after the loop terminates is undefined.  Similarly,
     BBIITT__FFOORREEAACCHH__IISSCCLLRR() iterates over all clear bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t.  In the loop
     body, the currently indexed bit may be set or cleared.  However, setting
     or clearing bits other than the currently indexed bit does not guarantee
     that they will or will not be returned in subsequent iterations of the
     same loop.

     The BBIITT__CCOOUUNNTT() macro returns the total number of set bits in _b_i_t_s_e_t.

     The BBIITT__SSUUBBSSEETT() macro returns true if _n_e_e_d_l_e is a subset of _h_a_y_s_t_a_c_k.

     The BBIITT__OOVVEERRLLAAPP() macro returns true if _b_i_t_s_e_t_1 and _b_i_t_s_e_t_2 have any com‐
     mon bits.  (That is, if _b_i_t_s_e_t_1 AND _b_i_t_s_e_t_2 is not the empty set.)

     The BBIITT__CCMMPP() macro returns true if _b_i_t_s_e_t_1 is NOT equal to _b_i_t_s_e_t_2.

     The BBIITT__OORR() macro sets bits present in _s_r_c in _d_s_t.  (It is the bbiittsseett((99))
